* Example input
Save as `response.json`; the recipes below use this shape.
@code json
{
  "data": {
    "items": [
      {"nftBuyId": "a1", "status": "ready", "amount": 12},
      {"nftBuyId": "b2", "status": "pending", "amount": 3},
      {"nftBuyId": "c3", "status": "ready", "amount": 20}
    ]
  }
}
@end

* Extract a field as raw text
@code bash
jq -r '.data.items[].nftBuyId' response.json
@end
`-r` removes JSON quotes around strings. For clipboard input on Wayland:
@code bash
wl-paste | jq -r '.data.items[].nftBuyId'
@end

* Filter items and select fields
@code bash
jq '.data.items | map(select(.status == "ready") | {id: .nftBuyId, amount})' response.json
@end
`map` returns an array; `.data.items[] | select(...)` emits separate results instead.

* Supply defaults for missing values
@code bash
jq '.data.items // [] | map(.label // "unnamed")' response.json
@end
`//` also replaces explicit null and false values.

* Pass shell values without interpolating JSON
@code bash
status=ready
jq --arg status "$status" '.data.items | map(select(.status == $status))' response.json
jq --argjson minimum 10 '.data.items | map(select(.amount >= $minimum))' response.json
@end
`--arg` passes a string; `--argjson` parses a JSON value, such as a number or boolean.

* Count items by status
@code bash
jq '.data.items | group_by(.status) | map({status: .[0].status, count: length})' response.json
@end

Related: {:shell:}[shell], {:wayland-clipboard:}[Wayland clipboard]. Reference: {https://jqlang.org/manual/}[jq manual].
